n many high-level matches, F6 is the go-to play.\n\nAfter F6, the opponent will likely respond, and it is probable it will be E6. My counter, after F6, would be D6, which would really hit back and strengthen my position. So F6 seems the soundest approach, considering future positioning and potential for board dominance. Also, it fits opening book theory.\n\nF6 it is. The answer is (F, 6).\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the very early opening phase (Move 3). I am playing as \'@\'. The opponent (\'*\') has played the "Perpendicular" opening move at F5, resulting in a cluster of opponent pieces at D5, E4, E5, and F5. I need to choose a move that counters this setup effectively by maximizing my mobility and limiting the opponent\'s options.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(D, 6)**: Places a piece horizontally. This would flip D5, creating a horizontal block of my pieces (D4-D5-D6). While stable, horizontal blocks in the opening can be easily flanked, and it leaves the opponent with good responses.\n- **(F, 4)**: Places a piece vertically. This flips E4, creating a vertical block (D4-E4-F4). Similar to D6, this creates a predictable shape that the opponent can exploit (e.g., with E6).\n- **(F, 6)**: Places a piece diagonally. This flips the central piece E5. This move creates a diagonal line of pieces (D4-E5-F6) which cuts through the opponent\'s formation. It prevents the opponent from solidifying the center and is the standard, theoretically strongest response ("book move") to the Perpendicular opening. It invites the opponent to play E6, which can be strongly countered by D6 in the subsequent turn.\n\nReason: Move (F, 6) is the best strategic choice. It breaks up the opponent\'s block of pieces by flipping the central disc (E5) and establishes a strong diagonal presence. This shape makes it harder for the opponent to enclose my pieces and preserves better long-term mobility compared to the block-building moves of D6 or F4.\n\nAnswer: (F, 6)'}
