id move. It blocks the horizontal line of opponent\'s * pieces. I\'m choosing a more "conservative" approach, focusing on D6 for now. This seems safe. It leaves D7 empty but blocks it from opponent. This move does not give the opponent a free opening. C6 also looks like a good choice to try to lock down Col 6.\n\nI\'m going with D6. It flips D5, the * piece, into one of my @ pieces. This is a very quiet move. I want to minimize frontier, take central squares, and avoid edges if possible.\n\nAfter analyzing, (D, 6) is a good decision: it\'s a solid central move that opens no new lines and limits the opponent\'s moves. There are no immediate risks from this move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the middle phase. The opponent (\'*\') controls the lower-right center (rows E, F), while I (\'@\') hold position in the upper-left center (rows C, D). The opponent has a strong block of pieces. My goal is to restrict the opponent\'s mobility while maintaining a stable position.\n\nEvaluation: \n1.  **(D, 6)**: This is a "quiet move". It flips only one piece (D5) and places a piece in a central position (D6). This minimizes the number of my pieces exposed to the opponent (min-maxing the frontier) and consolidates my control of row D.\n2.  **(C, 6)**: This move flips two pieces (D5, E4). While good, it extends into row C and potentially opens up more surface area for the opponent to attack than (D, 6).\n3.  **(F, 8)**: This move takes an edge position. While it flips four pieces, taking edges early in the game (especially non-corner edges) is often risky as it gives the opponent tactical targets (like wedging or corner access) and drastically increases the piece count (frontier).\n4.  **(E, 7)**: This move flips three pieces deeply but places a piece adjacent to the empty right edge, which is strategically weak (a "C-square" equivalent relative to the edge) and could allow the opponent to gain the edge later.\n\nReason: I chose **(D, 6)** because it is the most strategically sound move for this stage of the game. It is a central move that flips the minimum number of pieces (1), effectively limiting the opponent\'s future move options (mobility) while keeping my own frontier small and stable. It avoids the risks associated with taking edges prematurely.\n\nAnswer: (D, 6)'}
