res and X-squares like A2, B2, B7, G7, G8) which would give the opponent easy access to corners. My objective is to play a move that limits the opponent\'s mobility (minimax strategy) while maintaining a safe structure.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **(B, 4)**: This is a strong candidate. It is an internal move located at Row B, Column 4. It flips only one opponent piece at C4 (Direction South: B4 -> C4(*) -> D4(@)). Flipping few pieces ("quiet move") is advantageous as it limits the new moves created for the opponent. It does not touch the edges or corners.\n2.  **(G, 4)**: This move at Row G, Column 4 is also internal. It flips two pieces (F4, E4) vertically. While decent, it adds to the existing large cluster of \'@\' pieces at the bottom, which could become unwieldy.\n3.  **(A, 3)**: This is an edge move (Row A). It flips three pieces (B3, C3, D3). While taking edges is good, doing so early with a move that flips many pieces can be unstable and allow the opponent to wedge in.\n4.  **(C, 1)**: Another edge move. It flips D2. It\'s an acceptable alternative but generally, central moves like B4 are preferred to maintain influence before taking edges.\n5.  **Dangerous Moves**: (B, 2), (B, 7), (G, 7) are X-squares adjacent to corners. These should be avoided to prevent giving the opponent a corner.\n\nReason:\nI choose **(B, 4)** because it is the most stable "quiet move". By flipping only a single disc, it restricts the opponent\'s options for their next turn. It occupies a central position that does not weaken my structural formations and strictly avoids the tactical risks associated with playing near the corners (C/X-squares) or taking unstable edges prematurely.\n\nAnswer: (B, 4)'}
