eel pretty good about this.  Let\'s go with (D,7).\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the middle stage. I have a concentrated presence in the center and bottom-center, while the opponent controls much of the upper-left and right edges. The corners are all open. I need to play a move that preserves my mobility, avoids giving up corners, and does not flip too many pieces (evaporation strategy) to limit the opponent\'s options.\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **(D, 7)**: This is a quiet, internal move. It flips D6 and C6. It strengthens my position in the center-right without exposing me to corner attacks. While it allows the opponent to potentially take the edge at D8, that is not an immediate threat to the corners.\n2.  **(E, 8)**: This is an edge move. It flips F7 and G6. While it secures an edge spot, it flips F7 to my color (a C-square piece), which could be a liability for the H8 corner later.\n3.  **(C, 7)**: Similar to D7, but slightly less central. It flips C6 and D6.\n4.  **(A, 3)**: This flips a large number of pieces (column 3). While it gains material, it opens up the left side for the opponent and gives them high mobility, which is strategic disadvantageous at this stage.\n\nReason: I choose **(D, 7)** because it is a "quiet move" that improves my board structure without flipping too many opponent pieces. It maintains a stable frontier and forces the opponent to find a response, likely pushing them towards the edge (D8) where their mobility will be restricted, rather than giving them easy access to the center or corners.\n\nAnswer: (D, 7)'}
