nce in the center and towards the bottom right, while the opponent ('*') occupies the center-left. I have a list of valid moves, notably including the top-right corner at (A, 8).\n\nEvaluation: The move (A, 8) is the most promising. In Reversi, corners are the most valuable positions because pieces placed there can never be flipped. Taking (A, 8) will flip the opponent's piece at A7, creating a connected line of my pieces from A6 to A8. This secures the corner and establishes a strong permanent anchor on the top edge. Other valid moves, such as edge moves at (E, 8) or (F, 8), are decent but inferior to a corner. Moves like (G, 8) or (B, 2) involve placing pieces on C-squares or X-squares, which are strategically risky as they often surrender corners to the opponent.\n\nReason: My choice is (A, 8) because securing a corner is the fundamental winning strategy in Reversi. It locks in a stable piece that helps control the adjacent edges (Row A and Column 8) and limits the opponent's mobility in that sector. Since the move is available and safe (supported by my piece at A6), it is clearly superior to all other options.\n\nAnswer: (A, 8)"}
